WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.520
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:24.900
It's Friday.

9
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:25.820
It's not Wednesday.

10
00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:27.200
We're hitting your feed.

11
00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.280
On a Friday.

12
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.240
On a Friday.

13
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.540
T-G-I-F.

14
00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:32.440
We have a way to celebrate.

15
00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:33.860
We hope you like it.

16
00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:42.480
We are going to play for you the entire first chapter of our audiobook of The Office BFFs.

17
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.720
So happy Friday.

18
00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.000
Hang out with us for a little bit, and we hope you have a great weekend.

19
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:50.900
Chapter 1.

20
00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:52.660
Job Opening.

21
00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.080
Office Work.

22
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.860
In 2003, a group of strangers had one very big thing in common.

23
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.780
We had all just been cast on a new TV comedy pilot called The Office.

24
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:12.620
After years of living as struggling artists, we were all very happy to be employed in doing what we loved.

25
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:17.280
We could never have predicted the impact it would have on the rest of our lives.

26
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:23.630
I have an old photo from my first day of work on The Office.

27
00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:34.410
It was taken when Steve Carell, John Krasinski, Rainn Wilson, BJ Novak, David Denman, Phyllis Smith, and I met with the producers,

28
00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:41.350
network executives showrunner greg daniels and director ken kwapas to do a table read of the

29
00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:47.530
pilot script for the very first time allison jones our casting director came to the table

30
00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:53.750
read armed with a plate of homemade chocolate chip cookies and a polaroid camera i desperately wanted

31
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.330
a picture to commemorate the day and this was before everyone had cameras on their phones

32
00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.730
So I was excited when she told us all to gather together for a photo.

33
00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.490
I asked if she would take one for me, too.

34
00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:10.630
She said sure, took a second picture, and handed me the Polaroid and a cookie.

35
00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:16.930
I remember standing and munching the cookie while watching the photo slowly develop before my eyes.

36
00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:22.630
To let you know how special this whole moment was, let me share a little about my backstory.

37
00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.510
I grew up in St. Louis, Missouri.

38
00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:30.010
From the time I was seven years old, I knew I wanted to be an actor.

39
00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:35.410
I can't say why exactly, but I barely remember a time when this wasn't my goal.

40
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:43.730
At age nine, I considered becoming a veterinarian, but when I learned I would have to do surgeries on animals, I went back to my original plan of actor.

41
00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:49.550
My parents were very supportive, but also refused to let me act professionally as a child.

42
00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:53.290
And it's not like there were a ton of opportunities in St. Louis, to be honeSt.

43
00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.630
They told me I had to graduate from high school and college before moving to Los Angeles.

44
00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.650
So, I auditioned for every school play and joined every drama club.

45
00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.910
I spent most of high school in the dancing chorus,

46
00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.270
until my senior year when I was cast as the fiddler in The Fiddler on the Roof.

47
00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.850
I had no lines but extra solo dancing.

48
00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.570
I went to college at a small liberal arts school in rural Missouri

49
00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:21.410
called Truman State University and majored in theater.

50
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:26.770
I learned a lot about acting technique, and my love of performing was solidified.

51
00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:35.870
After graduation, I packed up my Mazda 323 hatchback and drove across the country to Los Angeles, ready to give it a go in Hollywood.

52
00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:42.830
I ended up spending years in various odd jobs, trying to pay off my defaulted credit card and make my rent.

53
00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:51.210
Thanks to a mandatory typing class in high school, most of the jobs I found were working as, wait for it, a receptioniSt.

54
00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:57.890
In the meantime, I was going on hundreds of auditions and performing Commedia dell'arte with a local theater company.

55
00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:05.150
I earned my Screen Actors Guild card doing background work, experience that would come in handy later, as you will learn in this book,

56
00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:08.510
and each year I seemed to do a little better than the year before.

57
00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:15.710
I booked some guest appearances on television shows, a couple of independent films, and a pilot called Rubbing Charlie.

58
00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:17.610
Yes, you heard that right.

59
00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:23.410
It was a comedy starring a stressed-out doctor named Charlie, played by Scott Wolfe,

60
00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:26.770
who gets life lessons from his massage therapist, me.

61
00:04:27.250 --> 00:04:29.050
I actually rubbed Charlie.

62
00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:31.930
It was a really sweet and funny show.

63
00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.210
I was convinced it would be my big break.

64
00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:38.850
So when it didn't make it past the pilot stage, I was crushed.

65
00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:44.470
I told my manager I wanted to quit, the years of rejection having taken their toll,

66
00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.370
and my new plan was to apply to vet technician school,

67
00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.630
animal care with no surgeries.

68
00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.150
She told me to keep at it,

69
00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.110
that the right role would find me if I just didn't give up.

70
00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:57.870
I had my doubts.

71
00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.890
At that point, I'd been plugging away in Los Angeles

72
00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:02.270
for nearly eight years,

73
00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:06.090
but I decided to take her advice and give it one more year.

74
00:05:06.710 --> 00:05:09.450
As fate would have it, the following pilot season,

75
00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.930
I was asked to audition for the American remake

76
00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:14.290
of a BBC show called The Office.

77
00:05:14.470 --> 00:05:17.330
The role was for the receptionist, Pam,

78
00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.310
a worn-down yet still hopeful young woman

79
00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.330
with artistic dreams beyond her depressing day job.

80
00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:25.330
In other words, me.

81
00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.050
After we snapped the shot, Steve Carell said,

82
00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.330
one day this photo will be worth money,

83
00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:33.170
especially after they fire me

84
00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:35.410
and replace me with a new actor for episode two.

85
00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.270
You'll all look at this and say,

86
00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.350
aw, remember Steve, what's his name?

87
00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:41.590
He was sweet.

88
00:05:41.830 --> 00:05:43.210
I wonder what he's up to now.

89
00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:47.950
Before The Office, Steve had the most experience of all of us.

90
00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:53.170
He wasn't a household name by any means, but he had been a correspondent on The Daily Show,

91
00:05:53.530 --> 00:05:55.610
he'd been a regular on The Dana Carvey Show,

92
00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:59.970
and he'd had a scene-stealing role opposite Jim Carrey in the movie Bruce Almighty.

93
00:06:00.550 --> 00:06:06.830
He had also been rejected by Saturday Night Live and been cast in more than one failed pilot.

94
00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:10.990
He knew better than all of us how rough and random our business could be.

95
00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:17.410
rain wilson had been knocking around for about 10 years traveling the country doing shakespeare

96
00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:23.230
and most recently worked as a recurring character on six feet under phyllis had spent the previous

97
00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:28.530
19 years working in casting including eight as a casting associate for allison jones

98
00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:35.650
when we all came in to audition for the office phyllis was our reader ken kwapas was so taken

99
00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:40.130
with her he told showrunner greg daniels he thought she should play a role on the show

100
00:06:41.090 --> 00:06:46.010
John Krasinski had done some commercials, and like me, his last pilot was not picked up.

101
00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:50.030
BJ Novak had been doing stand-up comedy when Greg discovered him.

102
00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:52.330
David Denman was like Steve.

103
00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:56.370
He'd been an employed yet mostly unknown actor for years,

104
00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:59.570
having appeared in a number of studio films and miniseries.

105
00:07:00.110 --> 00:07:04.170
He'd also done four pilots, none of which ever made it to series.

106
00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:07.970
We all laughed at Steve's joke, but the truth is,

107
00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:11.850
we were all also secretly wondering if we'd get to episode two.

108
00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:18.070
Also there that day were the creators of the original version of The Office,

109
00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:20.930
Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

110
00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:26.930
I had watched every episode of the British series and naturally was a huge fan.

111
00:07:27.590 --> 00:07:32.110
I was in awe of Lucy Davis's portrayal of Dawn, Pam's British counterpart.

112
00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.950
She could communicate so much with just a glance.

113
00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:41.270
I hoped that I could bring as much heart and depth to Pam as Lucy did to Dawn.

114
00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:46.230
As I sat with the creators of this masterpiece, I remember thinking,

115
00:07:46.750 --> 00:07:53.010
if I lose this job tomorrow, at least I got to sit in the same room as Ricky Gervais and Stephen Merchant.

116
00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:59.270
After the read, Ricky and Stephen gave us ideas for how to best Americanize the show.

117
00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:03.170
This had been the subject of a lot of discussion amongst the creative team.

118
00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:06.610
In England, they do much shorter television seasons.

119
00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:11.070
The original Office series was only 12 episodes long in total,

120
00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:12.790
with one Christmas special.

121
00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:16.310
American networks expect a lot more than that.

122
00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:21.450
A typical American television show makes 24 episodes per season.

123
00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:25.630
Ricky noted that if we hoped to make 100 episodes,

124
00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:30.030
we should be sure people didn't become too frustrated by Michael's incompetence.

125
00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.690
He suggested we make him a buffoon,

126
00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:34.270
but still good at his job.

127
00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:37.330
That way, American viewers would find something

128
00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.110
to admire in Michael amidst all the annoyance.

129
00:08:40.730 --> 00:08:42.390
Stephen Merchant then said

130
00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.030
that the Jim and Pam relationship

131
00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:46.890
was another way to balance Michael's obnoxious behavior.

132
00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:49.710
He said, always remember,

133
00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.430
Jim and Pam are the heart of the show.

134
00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.010
Gulp, no pressure.

135
00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.680
I did not grow up in Los Angeles either.

136
00:09:00.020 --> 00:09:04.060
I grew up in Indonesia by way of Louisiana and Texas,

137
00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.440
My dad was a drilling engineer, and we moved around for his job.

138
00:09:07.980 --> 00:09:10.180
I always knew I wanted to be a performer.

139
00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.920
My mom loves to tell the story about when she asked me and my older sisters what we

140
00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.240
wanted to be when we grew up.

141
00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.320
All my sisters had funny responses, but mine, she said, was the most puzzling.

142
00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:25.700
My sister, Billie, said she wanted to be, quote, the boss.

143
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:31.880
Janet wanted to be a truck driver like our Uncle Carl, Tina, a gymnast, and I wanted to

144
00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:32.700
be Carol Burnett.

145
00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:34.360
I was four.

146
00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:38.980
My dad had shown me The Carol Burnett Show, and all I wanted to do for the rest of my

147
00:09:38.980 --> 00:09:40.200
life was be a comedian.

148
00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:44.800
My first big break was getting cast as Mary in the Christmas play at school.

149
00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:48.780
Not a lot of jokes were written for Mary, but hey, it was a lead role.

150
00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.060
During college, I would write stand-up bits and try them out for my friends.

151
00:09:53.380 --> 00:09:57.100
I had grown up watching David Letterman's monologues and would even memorize some of

152
00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:57.740
his routines.

153
00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.380
I was desperate to learn more about comedy writing, and after four years at Baylor

154
00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:07.960
university and a bachelor's degree, I headed to New York City to intern for a late night with

155
00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:08.880
Conan O'Brien.

156
00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:14.760
When I interviewed for my position on Conan, I was told only two interns were ever

157
00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.920
on the stage during rehearsals and tapings, the writer's intern and the band's intern.

158
00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:20.920
All the

159
00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:22.440
other interns were up in the offices.

160
00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:26.060
I was determined to have access to those rehearsals.

161
00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:30.300
That's where Conan and Andy Richter and the writers would work out his stand-up routine

162
00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:30.900
and sketches.

163
00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.740
Well, the writer's intern position had already been filled, so I told them I wanted

164
00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.180
to be the band's intern.

165
00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:40.820
They asked me if I knew anything about music.

166
00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:42.460
I lied and said,

167
00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:45.060
of course, music is my life.

168
00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:49.680
I got the job and learned that if you make a friend at SIR,

169
00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:54.480
Studio Instrument Rentals, you can fake it until you make it as a music intern.

170
00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:59.340
I spent my weekdays watching Conan and my weekends taking acting classes.

171
00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:03.180
A friend of mine had given me Uta Hagen's book, A Challenge for the Actor,

172
00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:10.560
and I was lucky enough to take a workshop from Carol Rosenfeld at the acting school started by Uta Hagen herself, HB Studio.

173
00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:19.320
Watching Conan and studying at HB Studio boosted my confidence and I decided to move to Los Angeles to try to be a television actor.

174
00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:23.920
At this point, I was working two jobs and auditioning constantly.

175
00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:30.140
I had had a string of national commercials, most notably for Buick, Chrysler, and Lay's

176
00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:30.640
Wow!

177
00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:32.160
chips with Olestra.

178
00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:36.680
Yes, the chips that gave everyone abdominal cramping, diarrhea,

179
00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:37.920
and anal leakage.

180
00:11:38.760 --> 00:11:43.360
Between auditions, I was an operator at 1-800-DENTIST and ran the intern

181
00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.200
program at the iOS Comedy Club.

182
00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:48.440
I had been performing improv three nights a week for a

183
00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:48.840
decade.

184
00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.200
You know that annoying friend who was constantly handing you a flyer for their show?

185
00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:54.600
That was me.

186
00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:59.440
During this time, I was married to Warren Lieberstein and related by marriage to

187
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:00.260
Greg Daniels.

188
00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:05.320
Greg and his wife Suzanne, Warren's sister, were always very supportive and came to

189
00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.260
several of my shows.

190
00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:10.860
In the summer of 2003, Suzanne invited us over for a swim.

191
00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:12.660
I coveted

192
00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:18.500
these afternoon swims because our tiny apartment had only one AC window unit, and summers were

193
00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:18.960
rough.

194
00:12:19.480 --> 00:12:23.400
I had wedged myself into one of their daughter's brightly colored floaties and was

195
00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:27.080
trying to paddle to the steps of the pool when Greg sat down to chat.

196
00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:28.760
He told us he was going

197
00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:31.200
to be remaking the BBC version of The Office.

198
00:12:31.920 --> 00:12:35.540
I remember trying to suppress a uh-oh look on my

199
00:12:35.540 --> 00:12:35.940
face.

200
00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:41.080
A few other major networks tried to bring BBC shows to the United States and failed.

201
00:12:41.340 --> 00:12:46.220
The sensibility and tone of a show for a British audience had not transferred well to an American

202
00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:46.740
audience.

203
00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:52.420
I loved the BBC version of The Office and was worried that making it for an American

204
00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:53.840
audience would be a disaster.

205
00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.320
But I also felt that if anyone could pull it off, it would be

206
00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:57.640
Greg.

207
00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:02.620
He had been writing and producing on hit television shows for years and had won several

208
00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:07.420
awards for Saturday Night Live, The Simpsons, and a show he co-created, King of the Hill.

209
00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.040
Greg is one of the most brilliantly funny people you will ever meet on a set or off.

210
00:13:13.520 --> 00:13:17.380
He suggested I come in an audition because he thought that my improv background

211
00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:20.240
would lend itself to the mockumentary style of the show.

212
00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:24.060
Plus, he wanted to use as many unknown actors as he could.

213
00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:28.220
Greg felt that the world of a small paper company in Scranton, Pennsylvania

214
00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:32.260
would feel more organic if there were no big Hollywood names in the mix.

215
00:13:32.580 --> 00:13:35.560
I was definitely not a big Hollywood name,

216
00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.340
unless Lay's wow potato chip lady counted.

217
00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:43.580
I was very excited for the opportunity, but also incredibly nervous.

218
00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:48.540
Greg told me he would put my name on the list to audition, but with one caveat.

219
00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:50.820
No one could know that we were related.

220
00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:56.100
Greg felt strongly that letting this information get out to the network would only hurt my chances.

221
00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.160
We decided that we would not acknowledge each other at the audition.

222
00:14:00.680 --> 00:14:04.240
He could get me in the room, but I had to win the room over on my own.

223
00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:08.080
I later learned that this ruse was Allison Jones's idea.

224
00:14:08.360 --> 00:14:12.020
She was a big champion of mine and had told Greg this was the best plan.

225
00:14:12.500 --> 00:14:13.720
Thank you, Allison!

226
00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:20.320
On November 17, 2003, I auditioned for the role of Pam.

227
00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:22.720
Yep, fact.

228
00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.980
I signed into the audition at 2.40 p.m.

229
00:14:26.080 --> 00:14:30.900
and was the 16th person that day to read for the role of our favorite receptioniSt.

230
00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:34.240
I know this not because I have an amazing memory,

231
00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:39.160
but because the incredible Allison Jones saved my audition sign-in sheet.

232
00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:44.180
When the series wrapped nine years later, she gave it to me as a farewell gift.

233
00:14:44.500 --> 00:14:46.480
I ugly cried, of course.

234
00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:49.400
I treasure owning this piece of my life history.

235
00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.300
So many amazing people were there on that same day.

236
00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:57.120
Every female comedian I knew went in for the role of Pam.

237
00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:00.200
I remember my audition very clearly.

238
00:15:00.500 --> 00:15:04.960
It's weird how the brain captures the biggest moments of your life and slows them down.

239
00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:08.020
Every single detail is stuck in my brain.

240
00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:11.360
I wore a pink sweater and a black pencil skirt.

241
00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.280
I had worked really hard to prepare my scenes.

242
00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.360
I waited in a very small room before my audition.

243
00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.560
Catherine Hahn was sitting on a sofa in the corner.

244
00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:22.920
We smiled at each other and said hello.

245
00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:26.780
She is a doll and so talented, I tried my best not to get anxious.

246
00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.340
I am not sweaty by nature.

247
00:15:29.460 --> 00:15:31.860
I usually run cold no matter the temperature.

248
00:15:32.060 --> 00:15:35.140
But as it got closer to my turn, I really began to sweat.

249
00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:39.200
I actually went into the bathroom and dabbed toilet paper in my armpits.

250
00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:41.780
Sorry for the overshare, but that's how nervous I was.

251
00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.900
When it was finally my time, I walked into a room full of producers.

252
00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:51.900
There was a camera on a tripod, and sitting beside the camera was the casting associate, Phyllis,

253
00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.680
my future party planning committee rival.

254
00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:56.580
I was to read with her.

255
00:15:57.720 --> 00:16:01.360
Greg was sitting in the very back of the room, and neither of us made eye contact.

256
00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:05.060
My agent told me they were having everyone read two scenes,

257
00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.920
but if they liked you, they asked you to do a third.

258
00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:11.900
Phyllis and I read my first two scenes, and everyone was laughing.

259
00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:13.020
It felt great.

260
00:16:13.800 --> 00:16:16.420
Then I was asked to do a third.

261
00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.960
I worked hard to contain my excitement.

262
00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:21.440
Phyllis and I began the final scene,

263
00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:24.920
the one where Michael fake-fires Pam in front of Ryan the Temp.

264
00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:29.500
To jog your memory, in the scene, Michael wants to prank Pam

265
00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:32.860
by pretending to fire her for stealing post-it notes.

266
00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:37.380
The fake firing goes terribly wrong, and Pam is very upset.

267
00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:40.480
She starts to cry and calls Michael a jerk.

268
00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:42.380
The scene was going well.

269
00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.660
Phyllis was killing it as Michael.

270
00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:47.900
Imagine someone with Phyllis's sweet face

271
00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:50.500
smugly telling you that you've been punked.

272
00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:53.620
When we got to the part where I was supposed to tear up

273
00:16:53.620 --> 00:16:54.880
and call Michael a jerk,

274
00:16:55.120 --> 00:16:58.280
I really let Michael slash Phyllis have it.

275
00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:02.800
Now, there are three times you can tell I am from Texas.

276
00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:06.520
When I am tired, tipsy, or pissed off.

277
00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:10.460
So I definitely slipped into a southern drawl as I growled,

278
00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:11.220
Jerk!

279
00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:15.900
The room of producers burst out laughing, and I remember thinking,

280
00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:20.940
Hmm, not so sure I'm supposed to get a laugh at this point, but okay.

281
00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:22.640
And that was it.

282
00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:24.620
Allison thanked me for coming in.

283
00:17:24.620 --> 00:17:28.300
Greg discreetly smiled from the back row and gave me a thumbs up,

284
00:17:28.340 --> 00:17:30.780
and I walked out of there on cloud nine.

285
00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:32.900
I just knew I had that part.

286
00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:35.840
The next day, I got a call from Allison.

287
00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:37.700
I did not get that part.

288
00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:42.020
She told me they really liked me but thought I was a little too feisty for Pam.

289
00:17:42.580 --> 00:17:45.980
I was disappointed, of course, but I felt good about my audition,

290
00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:47.700
and I was thankful for the opportunity.

291
00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:51.000
So I let it go, and I moved on.

292
00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:53.280
That's just what you have to do as an actor.

293
00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:58.400
Plus, I had another audition that week for an improv pilot that I was really excited about.

294
00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:03.440
It was about a group of people working at a hair salon in New York City for the Oxygen Network.

295
00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:06.720
The working title was Salon Royale.

296
00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.580
I auditioned for the role of the promiscuous receptioniSt.

297
00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:12.600
Feisty was no problem here.

298
00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:14.760
And I was caSt.

299
00:18:15.640 --> 00:18:19.400
The producers of that show flew me out to New York City first class.

300
00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:19.500
class.

301
00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.760
I had never flown first class for work, and it felt so fancy.

302
00:18:24.420 --> 00:18:26.220
The cast was full of amazing

303
00:18:26.860 --> 00:18:31.160
improvisers—Ian Roberts, Beth Cahill, Dave Rozowski—and the show was written and directed

304
00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:32.200
by Emmy Laybourne.

305
00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:37.180
Emmy's mom, Geraldine, was the president of Oxygen at the time, so I thought

306
00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:39.000
we had a good shot of making it to series.

307
00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:43.460
But alas, we didn't get picked up, so back to Los

308
00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:44.500
Angeles, I went.

309
00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:49.580
After back-to-back rejections, I was feeling pretty discouraged about acting.

310
00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:54.520
I decided to concentrate my energy on writing and put acting on hold for a while.

311
00:18:55.260 --> 00:18:59.220
Several weeks went by, and I was feeling good about my decision to lean into writing.

312
00:18:59.800 --> 00:19:02.940
And then I got a call that changed everything.

313
00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:06.880
I was working the box office at iOS when I got

314
00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:07.940
a call on my flip phone.

315
00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:08.920
Remember those?

316
00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.980
It was a number I didn't recognize, so I let it go to

317
00:19:11.980 --> 00:19:12.540
voicemail.

318
00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:15.920
As it turns out, it was from Allison Jones's office.

319
00:19:16.420 --> 00:19:18.180
The producers of the office wanted

320
00:19:18.180 --> 00:19:21.980
to see me again for a new role, the prickly lady in accounting.

321
00:19:22.840 --> 00:19:24.520
Allison told me the creative team

322
00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:28.380
thought I was right for the world of Dunder Mifflin, just not quite right for Pam.

323
00:19:29.200 --> 00:19:29.900
This time,

324
00:19:30.020 --> 00:19:31.380
there was no big room full of producers.

325
00:19:31.380 --> 00:19:34.080
It was just the director, Ken Kwapis, Phyllis, and the

326
00:19:34.080 --> 00:19:34.420
tripod.

327
00:19:35.100 --> 00:19:39.780
I was told to come in wearing something drab, my hair plain, and with little to no makeup.

328
00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:43.660
Apparently, the prickly lady in accounting was no fashion plate.

329
00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:48.220
I wore a light blue turtleneck sweater with a gray cardigan and black pants,

330
00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:51.780
pulled my hair back in a low ponytail, and left my face bare.

331
00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:55.760
I channeled all my sass, and I got the part.

332
00:19:56.340 --> 00:20:01.660
And I was pretty sure that the prickly accountant would be an easier role for my mom to talk about

333
00:20:01.660 --> 00:20:05.900
at her lady's Bible study than the lustful salon receptioniSt.

334
00:20:06.100 --> 00:20:07.600
So everything worked out.

335
00:20:08.180 --> 00:20:12.440
I am so grateful to Greg for getting me in that room on November 17th.

336
00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:14.700
That moment changed my life forever.

337
00:20:15.240 --> 00:20:21.820
And little did I know, the gal that was cast in the role of Pam would become my best friend forever.

338
00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:29.240
Before we started taping the first episode, Ken Kwapis suggested we all personalize our desks.

339
00:20:29.620 --> 00:20:31.020
I loved this idea.

340
00:20:31.660 --> 00:20:34.020
Because of my real-life office experience,

341
00:20:34.020 --> 00:20:38.820
I had very strong feelings about the types of supplies a receptionist should have on their

342
00:20:38.820 --> 00:20:39.200
desk.

343
00:20:39.560 --> 00:20:43.300
The invitation to personalize my workspace made me very excited.

344
00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.180
Ken also suggested John

345
00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:49.880
Krasinski and I spend some time getting to know each other outside of work.

346
00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:51.420
Have lunch.

347
00:20:51.840 --> 00:20:52.220
Have

348
00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:52.760
coffee.

349
00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:57.500
Create a rapport, was the suggestion, as it will translate on screen.

350
00:20:58.060 --> 00:20:59.420
This is common for

351
00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.540
actors who are cast in a new project together.

352
00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:04.420
It's key to try and get to know the people you're

353
00:21:04.420 --> 00:21:08.780
going to be pretending to be in a relationship with so you don't seem like total strangers on

354
00:21:08.780 --> 00:21:09.160
screen.

355
00:21:09.820 --> 00:21:15.900
For example, during rehearsals for my job on ABC's Splitting Up Together, Oliver Hudson and

356
00:21:15.900 --> 00:21:20.080
I spent two days hanging out with the three children who would play our kids on the show.

357
00:21:20.700 --> 00:21:25.360
Like real parents, we sat reading each other bits of news articles off our phones while the kids

358
00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:25.840
wrestled.

359
00:21:26.440 --> 00:21:30.980
When I did the movie Hall Pass, I had drinks with Owen Wilson, who was playing my husband.

360
00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:37.840
Incidentally, I did not meet up with Scott Wolfe before rubbing Charlie, and, well, you already know

361
00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:38.600
how that worked out.

362
00:21:39.440 --> 00:21:43.220
Note, Scott Wolfe is a delightful human being, and I adore him.

363
00:21:43.320 --> 00:21:43.540
It is

364
00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:45.300
not our fault the show didn't go.

365
00:21:45.520 --> 00:21:46.500
We were fantastic.

366
00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:50.080
And I couldn't forget what Stephen Merchant had

367
00:21:50.080 --> 00:21:55.540
said, that John and I, virtual strangers, were to be the heart of the show.

368
00:21:56.340 --> 00:21:57.820
So one night after

369
00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:00.900
rehearsals, we decided to kill two birds with one stone.

370
00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:03.560
John Krasinski and I went to a nearby

371
00:22:03.560 --> 00:22:07.220
office supply store and shopped for things to decorate our desks.

372
00:22:07.640 --> 00:22:10.100
I got a label maker, message

373
00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:14.360
pad, message sorter, daily calendar, file folders, multicolored sticky notes.

374
00:22:14.620 --> 00:22:15.760
The list goes on and on.

375
00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:19.200
I left with two bags of supplies.

376
00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:23.120
I think John got a tape dispenser and maybe some post-it notes.

377
00:22:23.900 --> 00:22:28.620
The whole time we were shopping, we just kept talking about how excited we were to start

378
00:22:28.620 --> 00:22:29.020
shooting.

379
00:22:29.460 --> 00:22:32.880
We wanted to do everything we could to go from pilot to series.

380
00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:38.100
John also shared about moving from New York and the adjustment to living in Los Angeles.

381
00:22:38.600 --> 00:22:43.020
I was happy to learn that we were both pretty boring people, in a good way.

382
00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:44.960
He felt stable.

383
00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.420
He had a good work ethic, and he was funny.

384
00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:49.940
We laughed a lot.

385
00:22:50.560 --> 00:22:54.040
I could tell he was going to be a good acting partner.

386
00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:59.900
Greg had encouraged us to think about our characters and their backstories,

387
00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:01.720
so when it came to my desk area,

388
00:23:01.940 --> 00:23:05.740
I tried to imagine what a stuffy lady in accounting might bring to the office.

389
00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.900
I brought in a photo of my grandmother, Lena, and me.

390
00:23:09.200 --> 00:23:11.820
In the picture, my eyes are closed, but I am smiling.

391
00:23:12.320 --> 00:23:16.600
It makes me laugh that Angela Martin would choose to frame a photo where her eyes are closed.

392
00:23:17.280 --> 00:23:19.760
Like, perhaps that was just the best one of the bunch?

393
00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:22.020
I also brought in a day planner.

394
00:23:22.260 --> 00:23:26.400
I felt like Angela Martin would have an appointment book and it would be very comprehensive.

395
00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:31.240
The set dressers put a blue ceramic cat paperclip holder on my desk.

396
00:23:31.640 --> 00:23:35.960
The cat's left ear was slightly chipped, and I loved that little detail.

397
00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:40.520
It looked like a knick-knack you would get from a garage sale in a small town.

398
00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:45.320
That cat paperclip holder did a lot to inform who Angela Martin was.

399
00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:48.260
It was clear to me that she must love cats

400
00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:51.400
and that she didn't need fancy new things to make her happy.

401
00:23:51.720 --> 00:23:56.040
She was too humble for fancy, in a judgmental way, of course.

402
00:23:56.940 --> 00:24:00.580
People ask us all the time what we took from the set the day we wrapped the series,

403
00:24:01.060 --> 00:24:03.040
and I took that cat paperclip holder.

404
00:24:03.440 --> 00:24:05.740
It sits on my desk at home to this day.

405
00:24:07.860 --> 00:24:11.640
Walking onto the set for our first day of shooting, I was so nervous.

406
00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:14.400
I'd left my house at 5 a.m.

407
00:24:14.500 --> 00:24:16.440
for what was a 45-minute commute.

408
00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:21.640
I made myself a song mix I affectionately titled Songs of Scranton.

409
00:24:22.000 --> 00:24:24.920
I drank coffee and bounced along the freeway.

410
00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:30.820
I tried to imagine I was driving to my real job at a real paper company in Scranton, Pennsylvania.

411
00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:33.880
When I arrived, I was taken to wardrobe.

412
00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:40.780
There were a lot of very quick introductions to various other cast members who were coming and going between wardrobe, hair, and makeup.

413
00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:48.140
It was then that I first met Leslie David Baker, who would soon be known to many as Stanley Hudson.

414
00:24:48.740 --> 00:24:56.360
He was so warm, friendly, and open that watching him switch over to Stanley, the checked-out grump, was hilarious.

415
00:24:57.200 --> 00:25:03.020
Our director had requested that everyone be on set and at our desks at 7.30 a.m.

416
00:25:03.180 --> 00:25:03.360
sharp.

417
00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:07.540
He asked us all to pretend to work while the film crew recorded us.

418
00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:11.380
He wanted to capture us like real documentarians would,

419
00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:13.820
because this was the convention of the show.

420
00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:18.080
We were real office workers being filmed by a documentary film crew.

421
00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.340
When we were all in place, the set was cleared of all crew members

422
00:25:22.340 --> 00:25:25.740
except for the camera operator, boom operator, and director.

423
00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:30.960
They started rolling, and it all felt so real.

424
00:25:31.840 --> 00:25:33.820
It was very quiet at firSt.

425
00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:38.200
Then Phyllis and Leslie started improvising fake sales calls.

426
00:25:38.780 --> 00:25:41.680
I started to answer the phone and transfer calls.

427
00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:47.140
A lot of the shots you see in the opening credits of the show are from that very first morning.

428
00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.480
I was also a bundle of nerves that first day.

429
00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:56.300
My call time to set was 4.30 a.m., so I didn't get much sleep the night before.

430
00:25:56.680 --> 00:26:00.420
We were filming in an empty office building in Culver City, California.

431
00:26:00.880 --> 00:26:04.000
The building was only two stories, and we filmed on the second floor.

432
00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:09.020
There was an empty soundstage next to the main building where the hair and makeup team was set up.

433
00:26:09.380 --> 00:26:14.640
It was cold and dark when I arrived, with only one small space heater in that big drafty room.

434
00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:17.200
We huddled around it and waited our turn.

435
00:26:17.740 --> 00:26:22.180
Our director had instructed the hair and makeup departments to keep our looks very simple.

436
00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:26.000
I was given a loose French braid and some powder and lip balm.

437
00:26:26.320 --> 00:26:27.080
That's all.

438
00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:32.380
The wardrobe department asked me to bring the clothes and shoes I had worn to my audition for Angela Martin,

439
00:26:32.380 --> 00:26:35.080
so I actually wore my own clothes in the pilot.

440
00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:38.140
With barely any hair and makeup and no wardrobe

441
00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:40.380
fitting, it didn't take much time for me to get ready.

442
00:26:40.800 --> 00:26:43.560
I walked onto the set early and realized

443
00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:46.360
I wasn't sure where I was supposed to sit.

444
00:26:47.160 --> 00:26:49.540
Ken Quapis must have seen me looking around sort of

445
00:26:49.540 --> 00:26:54.240
loSt. He came over to me and told me I'd be sitting in the corner in what would become the

446
00:26:54.240 --> 00:26:55.100
accounting department.

447
00:26:55.680 --> 00:26:58.260
He walked me over to introduce me to my deskmates.

448
00:26:58.900 --> 00:26:59.780
There I found

449
00:26:59.780 --> 00:27:03.800
seated at the desk right next to me, Oscar Nunez.

450
00:27:04.200 --> 00:27:06.580
I had known Oscar for years.

451
00:27:07.120 --> 00:27:08.380
We first met at the

452
00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:13.120
Groundlings and then went on to perform together in a sketch comedy show called Hot Towel.

453
00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:14.160
I played

454
00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:16.560
a horny boy scout and Oscar a crazy professor.

455
00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:20.060
I was so thrilled to see my old friend that I yelled

456
00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.120
out, holy crap, Oscar.

457
00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:27.480
Neither of us knew that the other had been caSt. This was in the antiquated

458
00:27:27.480 --> 00:27:30.720
times before everything everyone is doing and saying was posted online.

459
00:27:30.980 --> 00:27:32.260
You could still be

460
00:27:32.260 --> 00:27:33.440
surprised by things.

461
00:27:33.900 --> 00:27:34.800
Imagine that.

462
00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:39.480
One of the very first photos I took on The Office set was of the

463
00:27:39.480 --> 00:27:40.340
accounting department.

464
00:27:40.520 --> 00:27:43.960
We took the photo in character, and it cracks me up that even in the

465
00:27:43.960 --> 00:27:47.800
first week, Oscar, Kevin, and Angela were clearly formed people.

466
00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:50.200
We already knew who we were in the

467
00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:50.960
world of the show.

468
00:27:51.220 --> 00:27:55.380
I think it speaks to how amazing the writing was on The Office, and also

469
00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:59.360
to Allison Jones and her gift for assembling acting ensembles.

470
00:28:01.170 --> 00:28:06.430
I'll never forget seeing Angela pop her head up over the partition between our desks to introduce

471
00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:06.890
herself.

472
00:28:07.270 --> 00:28:12.910
Her face barely made it over the edge as she said, hello, in her bubbly Texas twang.

473
00:28:13.310 --> 00:28:16.630
She was instantly funny, upbeat, and kind.

474
00:28:16.890 --> 00:28:18.350
I liked her immediately.

475
00:28:19.010 --> 00:28:21.110
She was clearly nothing like her

476
00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:24.430
character Angela Martin, which shows what a great actress she is.

477
00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:26.510
In addition to our chats

478
00:28:26.510 --> 00:28:31.370
over the partition, Angela would talk my ear off in the hair and makeup room, and she would keep

479
00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:34.010
talking as we walked to the snack table and then to set.

480
00:28:34.490 --> 00:28:37.030
I often joke that I had no choice but to

481
00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:40.250
become friends with Angela because she wouldn't stop talking to me.

482
00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:42.350
I was so grateful for her.

483
00:28:42.930 --> 00:28:45.770
I was nervous, and she was an instant comfort.

484
00:28:47.720 --> 00:28:50.160
My first memory of Jenna is hearing a little

485
00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:53.260
dainty sneeze come from behind the partition next to my desk.

486
00:28:53.620 --> 00:28:55.360
It reminded me of my neighbor's

487
00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:56.380
peek-a-poo sneezing.

488
00:28:56.660 --> 00:28:58.200
It was adorable.

489
00:28:58.980 --> 00:29:02.060
She sat directly behind me and was physically the closest

490
00:29:02.060 --> 00:29:03.240
gal to me on set.

491
00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:08.000
Jenna seemed shy, but then again, I am super chatty, and I probably scared her.

492
00:29:08.140 --> 00:29:09.440
I can do that to people.

493
00:29:10.080 --> 00:29:13.840
Every day that first week, I'd get up on my tippy-toes and pop my head over

494
00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:14.400
and chat.

495
00:29:15.000 --> 00:29:19.420
Those desk partition chats became our daily habit for the next nine years.

496
00:29:21.360 --> 00:29:24.060
The whole week seemed to speed by so faSt.

497
00:29:24.680 --> 00:29:31.980
Before we knew it, the pilot was done, and we all went home to wait the almost three months it took to learn if our show would make it to series.

498
00:29:33.180 --> 00:29:37.120
Shortly after we'd filmed the pilot, I went to London for a friend's wedding.

499
00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:42.020
While I was there, I reached out to Ricky and Steven to see if they were able to meet.

500
00:29:42.720 --> 00:29:48.420
I don't know where I got the courage to call, but since I'd basically been unemployed since shooting the pilot for the office,

501
00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:51.820
this was the one potentially exciting thing I had in my life.

502
00:29:52.180 --> 00:29:54.260
I wanted to milk it for all it was worth.

503
00:29:55.100 --> 00:29:59.140
They agreed to host me for lunch at the exclusive Groucho Club.

504
00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:03.160
The Groucho is a famed private club for comedians,

505
00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:05.160
and I couldn't believe I was there.

506
00:30:05.780 --> 00:30:08.020
I don't like to think too much about that lunch

507
00:30:08.020 --> 00:30:10.300
because I'm certain I sounded like a raving idiot,

508
00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:13.640
going on and on and praising their genius nonstop.

509
00:30:14.320 --> 00:30:15.960
They must have taken pity on me

510
00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:18.480
because they even agreed to pose for a photo afterward.

511
00:30:19.520 --> 00:30:23.980
After the lunch, I was sitting on the tube reading a discarded UK TV guide.

512
00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:29.760
Inside was an article about the casting of the American version of The Office.

513
00:30:30.200 --> 00:30:32.280
How crazy is that?

514
00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:34.860
The article was not positive.

515
00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:36.580
It said, quote,

516
00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:40.940
The chances of NBC actually matching wits with the original

517
00:30:40.940 --> 00:30:44.960
are about as good as finding a designated driver at a Wembley football match.

518
00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:45.740
End quote.

519
00:30:46.420 --> 00:30:52.600
It included photos of the original cast members alongside photos of each of us from the American version.

520
00:30:53.200 --> 00:30:58.420
The article basically went on to break down how we were ill-suited for the roles and would ruin the show.

521
00:30:58.860 --> 00:31:05.580
It ended by predicting our show would be as palatable as, quote, a plate of mushy peas, end quote.

522
00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:07.200
Huh.

523
00:31:07.200 --> 00:31:08.760
How did I feel about this?

524
00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:10.200
I'll tell you.

525
00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:13.340
My photo was in a magazine.

526
00:31:13.760 --> 00:31:15.540
I was blown away.

527
00:31:15.960 --> 00:31:19.640
It was the first time I'd ever been in a magazine.

528
00:31:20.140 --> 00:31:23.320
I tore out the article and tucked it into my purse.

529
00:31:23.760 --> 00:31:29.420
For years, I saved every press clipping about our show, even if I wasn't mentioned at all.

530
00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:35.300
I can't explain it exactly, but from the very beginning, this show felt special.

531
00:31:35.700 --> 00:31:37.540
It felt different from the reSt.

532
00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:44.340
I wanted to keep a record of every moment, even the ones where we were compared to mushy peas.

533
00:31:45.500 --> 00:31:51.540
about two months later greg called and said he'd finished editing the pilot episode and sent it off

534
00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:58.560
to the network he wanted to share it with the cast if the folks at uk tv guide were right it would be

535
00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:03.920
the only episode of the american version of the office ever produced so we wanted to celebrate

536
00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:09.740
i offered to host a viewing party it's something that would become an ongoing tradition when the

537
00:32:09.740 --> 00:32:13.480
show was on the air, we'd switch houses each week, watching it together.

538
00:32:13.700 --> 00:32:15.120
But this was the firSt.

539
00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:17.540
Everyone gathered at my house.

540
00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:20.660
I moved the furniture around in an effort to accommodate

541
00:32:20.660 --> 00:32:24.980
everyone, but there were so many people that folks were sitting on the floor, including Steve

542
00:32:24.980 --> 00:32:25.480
Carell.

543
00:32:25.980 --> 00:32:28.640
We ordered pizza and got set to watch.

544
00:32:29.420 --> 00:32:32.000
At first, you couldn't get your DVD player to work.

545
00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:34.880
Yes, I was mortified.

546
00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:39.460
Everyone was in my house, sitting on my floor, waiting to see this show

547
00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:40.840
that we'd worked so hard on.

548
00:32:41.000 --> 00:32:42.100
It was so much pressure.

549
00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:44.920
After some colorful language

550
00:32:44.920 --> 00:32:46.500
and trying a few different plugs,

551
00:32:46.740 --> 00:32:48.640
you changed the batteries in your remote

552
00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:49.580
and got it working.

553
00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:50.840
Night saved.

554
00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:53.340
And if I remember correctly,

555
00:32:53.620 --> 00:32:54.600
we watched it twice.

556
00:32:55.380 --> 00:32:58.000
We all told Greg how much we loved it.

557
00:32:58.260 --> 00:32:59.120
And we did.

558
00:32:59.640 --> 00:33:01.820
We felt good about what we had created.

559
00:33:02.480 --> 00:33:04.240
Greg told us he had no idea

560
00:33:04.240 --> 00:33:05.600
which way the network was leaning

561
00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:07.740
and that we'd have to wait another month

562
00:33:07.740 --> 00:33:08.560
to find out.

563
00:33:09.150 --> 00:33:09.860
A month!

564
00:33:10.320 --> 00:33:14.840
How in the world was I going to fill the next 30 days without going out of my mind?

565
00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:18.680
I decided to stalk more original Office cast members.

566
00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:24.300
My manager, Naomi Odenkirk, had a client who was shooting a pilot with Lucy Davis.

567
00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:27.160
I went to the taping and met her afterward.

568
00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:29.940
She was bubbly and smiley.

569
00:33:30.100 --> 00:33:34.320
She told me how the Office had changed her life and wished me all the same success.

570
00:33:34.820 --> 00:33:36.480
I asked for a photo, of course.

571
00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:38.740
Then I went home and waited.

572
00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:40.420
And waited.

573
00:33:41.340 --> 00:33:42.080
And waited.

574
00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:47.320
During our month wait, I went home to visit my family's farm in Texas.

575
00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:50.400
It's about 300 acres in the middle of nowhere.

576
00:33:50.920 --> 00:33:53.660
My dad would wake me up early in the morning to feed the cows.

577
00:33:54.100 --> 00:33:57.780
When I wasn't helping my dad, I was helping my mom and grandmother shell pecans.

578
00:33:58.640 --> 00:34:02.880
Hollywood felt very far away, and I was thankful for the distraction.

579
00:34:03.740 --> 00:34:08.800
While I was there, I got the news that NBC had picked up five more episodes of The Office.

580
00:34:09.120 --> 00:34:11.280
We were officially a series.

581
00:34:11.660 --> 00:34:13.500
My whole family was so excited.

582
00:34:13.760 --> 00:34:16.260
They knew what a long journey this had been for me.

583
00:34:16.800 --> 00:34:19.820
Mom said we should celebrate, so she put on a pot roaSt.

584
00:34:20.060 --> 00:34:22.380
A pot roast and homemade rolls.

585
00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:26.440
Birdie Joe, that's my mom's name, was going all out.

586
00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:30.940
Some meals you remember for your whole life, and that was one of them for me.

587
00:34:31.520 --> 00:34:34.840
Good news and a good pot roast, a celebration indeed.

588
00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:40.500
When I got the call that the show was getting picked up, I screamed.

589
00:34:41.080 --> 00:34:46.620
My very pragmatic agents congratulated me and then, I guess to temper my expectations,

590
00:34:47.140 --> 00:34:52.800
told me that five episodes was a very small order and it indicated very little confidence in the show.

591
00:34:53.240 --> 00:34:57.900
They were basically saying, have fun, but don't spend all your money in one place.

592
00:34:58.600 --> 00:35:02.640
In fact, my agents continued to send me other pilot scripts

593
00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:04.780
while we were shooting season one of The Office.

594
00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:07.740
A recurring theme of our early years

595
00:35:07.740 --> 00:35:09.220
was people explaining to us

596
00:35:09.220 --> 00:35:10.860
how our show would never really make it.

597
00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:14.740
I told them I was simply excited to be employed for now.

598
00:35:15.080 --> 00:35:17.500
I couldn't wait to get back to work with everyone again.

599
00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:21.000
Our pilot had been a direct adaptation

600
00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:22.520
of the British pilot script,

601
00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:26.240
but the network wanted the next five episodes to be original.

602
00:35:27.120 --> 00:35:32.000
Greg assembled a staff of writers to start breaking down stories for the first all-original

603
00:35:32.000 --> 00:35:32.700
episodes.

604
00:35:33.320 --> 00:35:39.520
That group was Mindy Kaling, Mike Schur, and BJ Novak, plus three consultants,

605
00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:43.120
Paul Lieberstein, Larry Wilmore, and Lester Lewis.

606
00:35:43.940 --> 00:35:46.040
One of the first stories they came up with

607
00:35:46.040 --> 00:35:49.820
was based on a real-life experience of their writer's assistant, Tom Huang.

608
00:35:50.360 --> 00:35:51.300
When Tom was

609
00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:56.160
in college, he took a class in which they did an exercise where they had to put note cards on

610
00:35:56.160 --> 00:36:01.260
their foreheads with different ethnicities, and then they had to go around and regard one another

611
00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:03.840
as that ethnicity using stereotypes.

612
00:36:04.660 --> 00:36:06.820
This turned into Diversity Day.

613
00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:10.520
BJ Novak, who was only 25

614
00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:15.820
years old at the time, was tasked with writing the episode, which the writing staff nicknamed

615
00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:17.760
the pilot after the pilot.

616
00:36:18.260 --> 00:36:21.780
It would establish what the audience could expect from the American

617
00:36:21.780 --> 00:36:23.700
conversion of the office moving forward.

618
00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:25.480
Good thing for us.

619
00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:26.600
BJ nailed it.

620
00:36:28.540 --> 00:36:29.640
The morning we filmed

621
00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:35.260
Michael Scott's Diversity Day seminar, the props department handed us our note cards and showed us

622
00:36:35.260 --> 00:36:36.860
how to place them on our foreheads.

623
00:36:37.120 --> 00:36:40.280
They were taped to our foreheads at 7 30 a.m.

624
00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:41.240
and we wore

625
00:36:41.240 --> 00:36:42.960
those cards for 12 hours.

626
00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:45.380
We only took them off at lunchtime.

627
00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:48.480
Hours went by and we forgot all about

628
00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:48.740
them.

629
00:36:49.180 --> 00:36:50.320
Mine said Jamaica.

630
00:36:50.920 --> 00:36:53.660
I wish I had taken a picture of us on our snack break.

631
00:36:54.040 --> 00:36:54.880
Imagine seeing

632
00:36:54.880 --> 00:36:58.540
the cast eating nachos with these ridiculous cards on their foreheads.

633
00:36:59.040 --> 00:37:00.700
Standing by the salsa bar was

634
00:37:00.700 --> 00:37:05.640
Jenna with Jewish taped to her forehead, talking to Brian with Italian taped to his.

635
00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:07.160
By the end of

636
00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:11.860
the day, the double stick tape had left skinny rectangular bald spots on our foreheads.

637
00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:12.800
But it

638
00:37:12.800 --> 00:37:13.360
was worth it.

639
00:37:13.640 --> 00:37:15.980
The cast was completely in sync.

640
00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:19.200
All our reactions every moment felt perfect.

641
00:37:19.800 --> 00:37:25.600
I remember looking around that conference room and thinking, oh my god, if we can just get people to

642
00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:27.500
watch the show, we're gonna be a hit.

643
00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:30.740
From the very beginning, it felt like the cast of The

644
00:37:30.740 --> 00:37:32.240
Office had a group mind.

645
00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:36.660
Bear with me, but this group mind idea is from the improv world, and I

646
00:37:36.660 --> 00:37:38.060
always relate back to that.

647
00:37:38.700 --> 00:37:42.680
By group mind, I mean we had this shared comedic collective

648
00:37:42.680 --> 00:37:43.440
consciousness.

649
00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:48.040
When you are performing improv as a group, no single person leads.

650
00:37:48.300 --> 00:37:49.480
The goal is to

651
00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:53.180
find your way together, listening, reacting, and being in the moment.

652
00:37:54.040 --> 00:37:55.860
I felt like our cast had

653
00:37:55.860 --> 00:37:57.580
this kind of comedic mind meld.

654
00:37:57.880 --> 00:38:01.240
The magic came from a combination of great writing and smart

655
00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:06.880
casting, but I think it was also due to the fact that the original cast shared a lot of history

656
00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:07.600
with one another.

657
00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:12.760
You already know that I knew Greg by marriage, and as a result, Paul as well,

658
00:38:12.880 --> 00:38:16.440
and that I had performed with Oscar in sketch comedy shows.

659
00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:18.800
But I also knew Kate Flannery,

660
00:38:19.040 --> 00:38:20.140
our saucy Meredith.

661
00:38:20.640 --> 00:38:23.500
For years, Kate and I performed improv together at I.O.

662
00:38:23.540 --> 00:38:23.920
WeSt.

663
00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:26.220
But wait, there's more.

664
00:38:26.920 --> 00:38:30.580
Jenna and I had been living a few blocks apart as struggling artists,

665
00:38:30.580 --> 00:38:33.780
using the same grocery store, but never met.

666
00:38:34.360 --> 00:38:38.100
Jenna, Phyllis, and director Ken Kwapis are all from St. Louis.

667
00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:42.520
Brian Baumgartner and Rainn Wilson had done regional theater together.

668
00:38:42.780 --> 00:38:44.420
Then there's the fact that B.J.

669
00:38:44.560 --> 00:38:45.880
Novak and John Krasinski

670
00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:48.380
were childhood friends in Newton, Massachusetts,

671
00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:52.500
and even played in the same Little League baseball division.

672
00:38:52.940 --> 00:38:54.780
Do you see what I mean here?

673
00:38:55.320 --> 00:38:58.140
For the most part, we were a group of strangers,

674
00:38:58.610 --> 00:39:02.980
Yet somehow, it felt like destiny that we would all come together on this show.

675
00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:07.260
You take these people whose lives have been crisscrossing for years,

676
00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:11.220
and you put them in a tiny little office, write them amazing scripts,

677
00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:13.240
and it was comedic lightning in a bottle.

678
00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:19.690
It's impossible to predict if a television show or movie project will be a success.

679
00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:25.250
And with The Office, we spent the first two seasons believing we could be canceled at any moment.

680
00:39:25.970 --> 00:39:28.510
No one looked at this group of people and said,

681
00:39:28.510 --> 00:39:35.590
bingo we've got a hit television show on our hands but what you did feel standing on that set

682
00:39:35.590 --> 00:39:41.730
all together was magic you could feel the chemistry of these people that greg had so

683
00:39:41.730 --> 00:39:48.370
masterfully gathered together and that includes our writers directors and crew every person on set

684
00:39:48.370 --> 00:39:54.470
was essential it's just as angela said it was as if we'd been crisscrossing one another our

685
00:39:54.470 --> 00:39:57.290
whole lives up until that point just for this purpose.

686
00:39:58.270 --> 00:40:00.970
Every role we hadn't gotten, every show

687
00:40:00.970 --> 00:40:05.410
before that didn't go, made it, so we were now together for this one.

688
00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:08.390
We had no idea if fans would

689
00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:14.510
like the show or how long the magic would laSt. We were all just grateful to be there creating

690
00:40:14.510 --> 00:40:15.410
something together.

691
00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:21.840
Thanks so much for listening to a little bit of our audiobook, The Office BFFs.

692
00:40:21.840 --> 00:40:25.460
You can buy the audiobook anywhere you buy audiobooks.

693
00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:30.740
And on the audiobook, we have some really fun friends who pop in for,

694
00:40:30.940 --> 00:40:32.240
I don't know, what would you call it?

695
00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:34.060
Surprise audio tidbits?

696
00:40:34.320 --> 00:40:36.520
I just want to say Ed Helms plays the banjo.

697
00:40:36.720 --> 00:40:40.900
And Creed Bratton wrote a special song that kicks off the whole audiobook.

698
00:40:41.340 --> 00:40:46.240
Rainn Wilson, he wrote and recorded a foreword just for the audiobook.

699
00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:47.820
Yeah, it's not in the hardback.

700
00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:50.540
Kate Flannery sings us a little jingle.

701
00:40:50.540 --> 00:40:54.020
And then we have all of these great moments with Steve Carell,

702
00:40:54.200 --> 00:40:56.440
Brian Bobgartner, Oscar Nunez,

703
00:40:56.740 --> 00:40:59.380
Ellie Kemper, and Jennifer Garner.

704
00:40:59.720 --> 00:41:01.420
Thanks so much for listening, you guys.

705
00:41:01.620 --> 00:41:02.800
We hope you have a great day.

706
00:41:03.160 --> 00:41:03.580
Bye.

707
00:41:03.820 --> 00:41:04.020
Bye.

708
00:41:04.540 --> 00:41:10.580
Thank you for listening to Office Ladies.

709
00:41:10.860 --> 00:41:12.880
Office Ladies is produced by Earwolf,

710
00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:14.760
Jenna Fisher, and Angela Kinsey.

711
00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:17.680
Our show is executive produced by Cody Fisher.

712
00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:20.080
Our producer is Cassie Jerkins.

